#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# elliptic_k

#% text
# `elliptic_k(m)` gives the complete elliptic integral of the first kind `K(m)`, the integral of `1/sqrt(1 - m*sin(t)^2)` for `t` from 0 to `pi/2`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The parameter is `m = k^2` (DLMF 19.2.8 with `k^2 = m`). `K(0)` is `pi/2`; `K(m)` has a logarithmic singularity at `m = 1` and is complex for `m > 1`.

#% item
# Machine numbers, balls and complex numbers give numerical values (by the arithmetic-geometric mean).

#% item
# `elliptic_k` is listable.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
elliptic_k(0)
#% output
#= (1/2)*pi

#% cell
elliptic_k(0.5)
#% output
#= 1.8540746773013719

#% cell
elliptic_k(-1.0)
#% output
#= 1.3110287771460598

#% cell
n(elliptic_k(1/2), 30)
#% output
#= 1.854074677301371918433850347195260

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# The period of a pendulum of length 1 m released at 120 degrees, `4*sqrt(L/g)*K(sin(a/2)^2)`:

#% cell
4*sqrt(1/9.81)*elliptic_k(sin(pi/3)^2)
#% output
#= 2.754089828887826

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The series in `m`, `pi/2` times the sum of `(binomial(2k, k)/4^k)^2*m^k`:

#% cell
pi/2*sum((binomial(2*k, k)/4^k)^2*0.36^k, [k, 0, 200]) - elliptic_k(0.36)
#% output
#= -6.661338147750939e-16

#% text
# The integral definition:

#% cell
n_integrate(1/sqrt(1 - 0.5*sin(t)^2), [t, 0, pi/2]) - elliptic_k(0.5)
#% output
#= 2.220446049250313e-16

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# `m = 1` is the singularity:

#% cell
elliptic_k(1)
#% output
#! failure(pole, elliptic_k(1))

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `elliptic_e`, `elliptic_f`, `elliptic_pi`, `jacobi_sn`
